ty-six  thousand and the switch 1.5 million now  there are many factors at play here but  as someone who has loved Nintendo for  most of my life and felt as if it lost  its way during the Wii U years I want to  point to three things it's doing  amazingly well first off its reshaping  its roots while many law didn't endow  for releasing amazing games in two of  its most legendary series I think it's  even more impressive how it has adapted  its origins for modern palates breath of  the wild isn't just one of the best  Zelda games but I would argue it's the  best open-world game ever made metroid  Santa's Returns doesn't just recapture  the series sense of exploration but it's  one of the best 2d Metroidvania style  games in a time when they release every  few months  what's more Nintendo seems to be  remoulding its roots with Mario as well  it hasn't released yet but from the 2  hours I've played of Super Mario Odyssey  it's reframing how its world works  giving us explorable sandboxes rather  than discrete levels to jump our way  through Nintendo isn't afraid to give  its iconic franchises massive overhauls  secondly Nintendo is working well with  third-party developers whereas the  company seems sheltered during the Wii U  years it's been coming out of it's shell  lately look no further than Mario +  rabbits Kingdom battle in one of the  most unlikely marriages ever Nintendo  has partnered with Ubisoft to bring us a  turn-based tactical combat game with the  Italian icon and the eccentric rabbits I  never thought I would see Mario rabbits  and XCOM combined but here we are and  it's one of my favorite releases of the  year and if you had told me one year ago  that Nintendo would partner with another  developer to revive the classic 2d  Metroid formula I would have laughed but  it did and not only as mercury steams  reimagine Metroid to amazing but if it  sells well it could make the case for  more 2d metroids down the road and of  course we also have rocket League 